---
// DRAFT, not published: Astro does not route files whose name starts with "_".
// To publish: rename to poolproof.astro, add it to the sitemap check, and set
// openSource.poolproof.caseStudy in src/config to { status: "published", href: "/work/poolproof" }.
//
// Facts here come from the poolproof README, src/data/poolproof.json and the
// public issue threads (pgdog#1849, supavisor#1239). Those bugs were reported by
// other people; poolproof reproduces them. My part in #1849 is the fix branch
// linked below, which was not merged when this was written.
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Matrix from "../../components/Matrix.astro";
import data from "../../data/poolproof.json";

const { trace, counts, scenarios } = data;
const targets = data.groups.reduce((n, g) => n + g.targets.length, 0);
const sql = (action: string) => action.replace(/^[a-z]+:\s*/, "");
const pooler = trace.target.split(" ")[0];

// The trace as lines of text, each with the class that colours it
const lines = trace.steps.flatMap((step, i) => {
  const out = [
    { cls: "is-dim", text: `${i + 1}  ${step.actor.padEnd(15)}` },
    { cls: "", text: `${sql(step.action)}\n` },
  ];
  if (step.differs) {
    out.push(
      { cls: "is-dim", text: "   Postgres       " },
      { cls: "is-good", text: `${step.postgres}\n` },
      { cls: "is-dim", text: `   ${pooler.padEnd(15)}` },
      { cls: "is-bad", text: `${step.pooler}\n` },
    );
  } else {
    out.push({ cls: "is-dim", text: "   both           " }, { cls: "", text: `${step.postgres}\n` });
  }
  return out;
});
---

<CaseStudy
  title="What a connection pooler changes"
  dek="Poolers are meant to be invisible to the application. poolproof checks that claim: it runs the same small scenarios against Postgres and through a pooler, and reports every step where the answers differ."
  source={{ label: "The repository on GitHub", url: "https://github.com/Nihal-Pandey-2302/poolproof" }}
  meta={[
    { text: "Nihal-Pandey-2302/poolproof", href: "https://github.com/Nihal-Pandey-2302/poolproof" },
    { text: `${data.repo.release}, released 4 Nov 2026` },
    { text: "Rust" },
    { text: "results site", href: data.source },
  ]}
>
  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      PgBouncer, PgDog, Supavisor, PgCat and Odyssey sit between an application and Postgres and share a few server
      connections among many clients. In transaction mode a client only holds a server connection for the length of a
      transaction, so anything that lives on the connection instead of in the transaction is at risk: a
      <code>SET</code>, a temp table, a prepared statement, an advisory lock.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each pooler documents some of this. Nobody had a way to check, for a given pooler and version, which of these
      actually break and how.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Postgres as the oracle</h2>
    <p>
      A poolproof scenario is a short script of steps from one or more clients. It runs twice: once against Postgres
      directly, once through the pooler. Every step's result is compared. Postgres is the reference, so no scenario
      hardcodes the answer it expects, and the same scenario works on any Postgres version.
    </p>
    <p>One scenario, as it ran against {trace.target}:</p>
    <figure class="cs-figure">
      <div class="cs-panel cs-term">{lines.map((line) => (
        <span class:list={[line.cls]}>{line.text}</span>
      ))}</div>
      <figcaption>
        multi-statement-begin. Postgres runs all three statements; through {pooler} the client gets back only the
        BEGIN, and a direct check afterwards finds no row.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Four kinds of failure</h2>
    <p>A difference is not just a failure. It matters how the application finds out:</p>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li><strong>Silent</strong>: the pooler answers, and the answer is wrong. The application has no way to know.</li>
      <li><strong>Error</strong>: the pooler refuses. The application finds out, at least.</li>
      <li><strong>Hang</strong>: no reply at all, until something times out.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Documented</strong>: a failure the pooler's own docs describe. Still a failure, because an app that
        relies on the feature still breaks, but not a bug.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>The results</h2>
    <p>
      On Postgres {data.postgres}, {scenarios.length} scenarios across {targets} pooler versions and configurations:
      {counts.pass} pass, {counts.silent} fail silently, {counts.errorOrHang} error or hang, and {counts.expected} are
      documented limitations. Select a cell to see what it checks and where the answers split.
    </p>
    <div class="cs-wide">
      <Matrix />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>What it found</h2>
    <ul class="cs-list">
      <li>
        PgDog 0.1.50 to 0.1.62 answers only the <code>BEGIN</code> of <code>BEGIN; INSERT; COMMIT</code> sent as one
        string, and never commits the insert if the client disconnects
        (<a href="https://github.com/pgdogdev/pgdog/issues/1849" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">pgdog#1849</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Supavisor 2.9.13 hangs on four pipelined requests in 9 of 10 runs
        (<a href="https://github.com/supabase/supavisor/issues/1239" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">supavisor#1239</a>).
        2.10.0-rc.1 answers them.
      </li>
      <li>
        PgBouncer 1.26.1 on Postgres 18 drops a client that set an empty <code>search_path</code> with
        <code>set_config()</code>, once another client has changed it
        (<a href="https://github.com/pgbouncer/pgbouncer/issues/1663" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">pgbouncer#1663</a>).
      </li>
      <li>Every pooler but Odyssey, with default settings, keeps a session advisory lock after the client that took it disconnects.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In all, it reproduces 14 upstream bugs, and the results show the version where each behaviour broke or got
      fixed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>From a red cell to a fix</h2>
    <p>
      The PgDog bug is the clearest case of why silent failures matter. On the issue, a team reported running a
      migration through PgDog as one multi-statement transaction and disconnecting. The client saw success. Neither
      the <code>ALTER TABLE</code> nor the <code>UPDATE</code> ran.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cause is in how PgDog routes a multi-statement query on a single shard: by its first statement only. When
      that statement is <code>BEGIN</code>, <code>COMMIT</code> or <code>ROLLBACK</code>, PgDog answers it itself, and
      the rest of the string is either replayed later with its results thrown away, or dropped.
    </p>
    <p>
      I posted a fix on the issue: in that case the whole string goes to the server unchanged, which is what already
      happens with PgDog's query parser off, so every result and error reaches the client. It comes with a unit test
      and an integration test that fails on main
      (<a href="https://github.com/Nihal-Pandey-2302/pgdog/tree/fix/multi-stmt-begin" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="link">branch</a>).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Before it reaches production</h2>
    <p>
      <code>poolproof scan</code> reads a <code>pg_stat_statements</code> export, Postgres logs or a
      <code>.sql</code> file, parses each query with the real Postgres parser, and flags the ones that break under
      transaction pooling, checked against what that pooler version actually did in the runs above. It writes SARIF,
      so findings show up in code scanning, and the repository doubles as a GitHub Action.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section">
    <h2>Not covered yet</h2>
    <p>
      RDS Proxy and hosted poolers are not in the test bed; the docs have recipes for testing them by hand. Session
      mode, a larger pool and five non-default settings run nightly on Postgres 18.4 only.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  /* the results grid needs the page's full width more than the reading column */
  .cs-wide {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
</style>
